xfmt for OS-9/68000===================ORIG holds comp.sources.unix volume 16, issue 71, xfmt, as posted.  Thefiles beside it build the OS-9 program.  lex.yy.c was made on OS-9 fromxfmt.l with the disk's `flex -S/dd/LIB/flex.skel'.The post asks for flex's skeleton to be patched (flex.skel.diff), so thata program can choose a start condition in main() before the firstyylex().  THAT PATCH IS NOT NEEDED HERE: the disk's flex.skel is skeleton2.16 and already carries both halves of it -- `static int yy_start = 0'and `if ( ! yy_start ) yy_start = 1'.  Vern Paxson took the changeupstream between the 1988 flex the post was written for and this one.flex.skel.diff is kept in ORIG and is not applied.What changed, and why:  xfmt.l     Seventeen comments in the rules section begin in column 1,             where this flex reads them as rule patterns and says             "unrecognized rule"; each is indented by a tab.  A comment             inside an action block is C and was left alone.             The definition `wh [ \t]*/[^a-z]' is used by fifteen rules.             This flex expands a {name} inside parentheses, and trailing             context is not legal there, so each use is written out in             the rule instead.  The patterns are unchanged otherwise, and             {wh} always stood at the end of its rule.             This flex's yyin is null until the scanner initialises             itself on the first yylex(), so main()'s             freopen(argv[optind], "r", yyin) had no stream to reopen: a             named file scanned nothing and printed nothing, with no             error, while standard input worked.  Under OSK yyin is given             stdin before the loop, which is what the 1988 flex declared             it as.  yywrap.c   The lex library, -ll on Unix, supplied yywrap(); the disk's             flex skeleton does not, so this does.getopt(), strchr() and strrchr() are not in this C library; SRC/unixlibsupplies all three and they build unchanged.  The program's own`#include <string.h>' is answered by SRC/COMPAT.Measured under os9exec, on a four-line ragged file: `xfmt rag.txt' refillsit to 72 columns, `xfmt -l 30 rag.txt' to 30, two files named together areeach refilled in turn, and standard input works with no file named.`xfmt -m DOC/xfmt/xfmt.1' renders the program's own manual page, headingsand indented option paragraphs included.  An unknown option prints theusage line and exits 1.Build: cc lex.yy.c yywrap.c ../unixlib/getopt.c ../unixlib/strchr.c          ../unixlib/strrchr.c -DOSK -l=/dd/LIB/termlib.l